Benign Fasciculation Syndrome
Idiopathic & Benign Entities
Spontaneous visible muscle twitching, Absence of objective muscle weakness, Normal deep tendon reflexes, Normal EMG

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: IBE-002
Reason for Visit: Acute health anxiety and distress secondary to widespread, continuous muscle twitching requiring urgent neurological assessment.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated severe, escalating panic regarding sudden-onset, widespread muscle fasciculations, with the patient expressing acute fear of impending paralysis (ALS). Urgent after-hours assessment was required to perform a comprehensive neurological examination, definitively exclude objective motor weakness or acute upper motor neuron signs, and prevent a severe psychological crisis or unnecessary ambulance dispatch prior to the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My muscles are twitching everywhere, I read it's ALS and I'm terrified I'm going to stop breathing."
History of Present Illness: Patient presents with frequent, visible, and spontaneous involuntary muscle twitching primarily in the calves and thighs, evolving over the past 3 days. The fasciculations are accompanied by a sensation of internal vibration and severe muscle stiffness. The symptoms sharply intensified following a period of overwhelming work fatigue and excessive caffeine consumption. Patient is fixated on catastrophic outcomes.
Functional Status: Independent, but pacing anxiously and unable to rest.
Social/Safety Concerns: Lives alone; significant acute health anxiety and rumination.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 135/82 | HR: 105 | Temp: 36.6 | RR: 20 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 81 Kg
General: Alert, visibly anxious, diaphoretic, pacing the room.
Physical Exam: Frequent, spontaneous visible muscle twitching noted in bilateral gastrocnemius muscles. Complete absence of actual, objective muscle weakness on formal resisted motor testing (5/5 globally). Intact, normal deep tendon reflexes (2+) without hyperreflexia. Complete absence of upper motor neuron signs; negative Babinski reflex bilaterally. No evidence of muscle atrophy or wasting. Normal baseline sensory perception.
Environmental Observations: Five empty energy drink cans visible on the desk.
Medication Reconciliation: No regular prescription medications. High recent intake of caffeinated beverages.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Benign Fasciculation Syndrome - Status: Exacerbated by anxiety and stimulants
Differentials:

Amyotrophic Lateral Sclerosis (ALS) - Ruled out due to complete absence of objective weakness, normal reflexes, and no muscle atrophy.

Electrolyte Imbalance (Hypomagnesemia/Hypocalcemia) - Unlikely given absence of true cramping, spasms, or Chvostek/Trousseau signs, though requires routine lab confirmation.

Thyrotoxicosis - Ruled out due to absence of goiter, normal temperature, and lack of resting tremor (aside from focal fasciculations).
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient required an urgent after-hours home visit due to acute, severe psychological distress and panic directly resulting from sudden-onset, widespread muscle fasciculations. The patient's acute fixation on a fatal neurodegenerative diagnosis created an immediate crisis that could not wait until morning. A detailed, immediate neurological examination was mandatory to clinically rule out acute denervation, upper motor neuron signs, and objective weakness. Confirming the benign nature of the clinical signs allowed for immediate de-escalation of the patient's panic, precluding an imminent and inappropriate emergency department presentation. This fulfills the requirement for urgent after-hours intervention (MBS Item 597).

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Immediate cessation of all caffeine and stimulants. Advised oral hydration. Non-urgent routine bloods (UEC, CMP, TFTs) via regular GP.
Positive Behavioral Change: Replace energy drinks with decaffeinated herbal tea or water. By successfully substituting the beverage, the physical trigger for the twitching is removed, and the subsequent reduction in fasciculations will positively reinforce the ongoing avoidance of high-dose caffeine.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Routine GP follow-up in 1-2 weeks for baseline bloods and reassurance.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate ED presentation if developing actual inability to lift limbs, severe difficulty swallowing, slurred speech, or shortness of breath.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Clinical examination is benign today. Persistent symptoms warrant routine blood tests and potential non-urgent nerve conduction studies (EMG) via your regular doctor to provide definitive reassurance.
Clinical Course: Benign fasciculations often persist for months or years but frequently wax and wane. They are entirely harmless and typically improve once stress, fatigue, and stimulants are reduced.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek immediate care if you experience genuine weakness (e.g., foot drop, unable to hold a cup), difficulty swallowing, or changes to your speech.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Your nervous system is currently hyper-excitable due to fatigue and caffeine, causing the nerves to randomly fire and twitch the muscles. This is a functional overactivity, not a structural disease.
Actionable Steps: Completely stop caffeine intake starting immediately. Prioritize 8 hours of sleep. The reduction in twitches over the next week will prove the benign nature of the symptoms and reinforce your new sleep/diet routine.

